--- body ---
## Context

`LocalEmbeddingProvider` calls `pipeline('feature-extraction', this.model)` with
no options object (`src/features/search/embeddings.ts:182`). transformers.js
therefore applies its own defaults, and on Node those are `DEFAULT_DEVICE_DTYPE:
"fp32"` and the CPU provider. The shipped local path runs
`Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2` at full precision on the CPU — the most expensive rung
of the ladder, reached by omission rather than by choice. ~~The model is already
configurable (`ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_MODEL`), so the asymmetry is the point: the axis
that costs the most is the one with no knob.~~

> **THE STRUCK SENTENCE IS FALSE, and it was this ticket's whole motivation.**
> The local path hardcodes `Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2`;
> `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_MODEL` reaches only the API providers, as the comment at that
> call site says. Neither axis has a knob, so the asymmetry never existed and the
> remedy it justified — a standalone precision knob — is withdrawn (log
> 2026-08-29, `tickets/0440`). The first sentence above survives: the shipped path
> does run at the most expensive rung by omission, and that is worth fixing. The
> fix is a model registry, not a knob.

Measured on this repo's corpus, ONNX CPU, i5-8250U, batch 1, one process per
rung (`bench/results/0025-x1-recall/dtype-ladder-nomic.json`):

| dtype | download | RSS | query median |
|-------|----------|-----|--------------|
| fp32  | 522,0 MB | 884,9 MB | 30,3 ms |
| q8    | 130,9 MB | 235,2 MB | 18,0 ms |
| int8  | 130,9 MB | 232,9 MB | 13,7 ms |
| uint8 | 130,9 MB | 239,5 MB | 14,0 ms |
| q4    | 157,5 MB | 247,4 MB | 25,2 ms |
| bnb4  | 150,7 MB | 253,7 MB | 91,4 ms |

Three findings shape what this ticket should and should not do.

**The ladder is not monotone, so "expose the knob" must not become "recommend
the lowest rung".** q4's published file is LARGER than q8's, holds more memory,
and is slower; bnb4 is 5,1x slower than q8 and 3x slower than full precision,
having no optimised CPU kernel. The same shape reproduces on
Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (`dtype-ladder-qwen3-06b.json`): q4 at 871,8 MB downloaded
and 1 355,8 MB resident against q8's 585,1 / 962,1, and bnb4 8,7x slower than
q8. Two models, one shape, so this is a property of the ONNX CPU runtime rather
than one model's graph. Eight bits is the knee and every rung below it is
dominated on every axis.

**`q8` and `int8` are the same file** on nomic-768 (md5 `b65952d0…`), where
`uint8` is a genuinely distinct blob. Measuring both therefore measured one file
twice and gives this campaign's noise floor directly: ±2,3 MB of RSS and ±4,3 ms
of median latency. Any recommendation that leans on a difference smaller than
that is reading noise.

**Two rungs do not load at all on the CPU provider.** `fp16` and `q4f16` abort
at session init with `Attempting to get index by a name which does not exist:
InsertedPrecisionFreeCast_/emb_ln/Constant_output_0 for node
/emb_ln/Mul/SimplifiedLayerNormFusion` — a graph-fusion failure, not a missing
kernel. So a `dtype` knob that accepts the runtime's full enum will hand users a
value that crashes on their hardware and works on someone else's, and the
failure must degrade rather than abort the server.

## Why device is not a knob

> **SUPERSEDED IN PART, 2026-08-29.** The section below argues from ORT's
> execution-provider fallback. That fallback was read from `onnx.js` and never
> run; run, `device: 'auto'` FAILS on a CPU-only linux-x64 machine rather than
> falling back. The ruling that device is never a knob is untouched — what is
> void is the belief that `auto` is a safe unconditional value. Read
> `verification/DEVICE-AUTO-0220.md` before acting on anything below, and see
> DECISIONS.md's awaiting-ratification entry for the three shapes the device
> can take from here. Action 2 is not implemented.

Ruled by the author 2026-08-29: the device is always `auto` and is not
configurable. Read `src/backends/onnx.js` in `@huggingface/transformers` 4.2.0
for why that is the strong choice rather than merely the tidy one.

`defaultDevices = ['cpu']` on Node. That single line is the whole of upstream's
current behaviour: passing no options gets CPU, on every machine, forever.
`deviceToExecutionProviders` then has `case 'auto': return supportedDevices`,
and `supportedDevices` is built per platform — Linux x64 `['cuda', 'webgpu',
'cpu']`, Windows `['dml', 'webgpu', 'cpu']`, macOS `['coreml', 'webgpu',
'cpu']`.

So `auto` does not resolve to one device. It hands ONNX Runtime the whole
ordered provider list and lets ORT's own execution-provider fallback walk it.
That is why no escape hatch is needed for the ordinary failure — an absent or
unusable provider is skipped by the runtime, not by our code — and it is why
action 5's degradation is a dtype concern only. It also means macOS gets CoreML
and Windows DirectML for free, which no CUDA-shaped knob would have delivered.

This is the axis where hardware changes what is reachable at all: the corpus
that projects to 56,5 h of CPU embedding at nomic-768 projects to 0,34 h on an
A4000 (`gpu-feasibility.json`). Note the asymmetry — the GPU is a BUILD lever,
not a query lever, because at batch 1 a small encoder is already at a few
milliseconds and there is nothing left to parallelise.

**Two things this ruling costs, recorded so they are visible rather than
discovered.** First, it is the one part of this ticket that is NOT
behaviour-preserving: today every user is on CPU, and after this change a user
with a GPU silently moves to it. Vectors shift in the last bits. That is
tolerable precisely because device is identity-neutral (action 4), but it should
be verified with the 0,999 probe and not assumed. Second, ORT's fallback covers
"provider unavailable"; it does not cover "provider present, registers, and then
misbehaves" — a broken CUDA install. With no knob there is no manual override
for that case. Do not add one speculatively: if that failure is ever observed in
the wild, it is the evidence that earns the knob.

**The defaults are the maintainer's call, not ours.** This ticket ships the
mechanism and the evidence and proposes nothing about what `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_*`
should default to. Changing a default changes every existing user's index
identity — vectors carry an embedder key, and a dtype change is a model change
for that purpose — so it is a decision with a migration attached, and it belongs
to him. Our contribution is that he can make it, and that the numbers to make it
with are on the table.

## Relevant files

- `fork/src/features/search/embeddings.ts` — `LocalEmbeddingProvider.ensure()`
  at ~:182 is the single call to change; `EmbedderKey`/vector-space identity
  lives in the same file (~:25) and is what a dtype change must be reflected in
- `fork/src/config.ts` — `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_MODEL` at :288 is the pattern to
  follow for the one new variable; the zod schema at ~:151 is where it parses
- `fork/src/features/search/limits.ts` — `DEFAULT_EMBED_BATCH_SIZE`, the
  existing example of a tunable with a named default
- `bench/query_embed_cost.mjs`, `bench/quant_fidelity.mjs`,
  `bench/quant_fidelity_score.py`, `bench/merge_dtype_ladder.py` — the drivers
  that produced the evidence; they already take `--dtype` and `--device`
- `bench/results/0025-x1-recall/dtype-ladder-*.json`, `query-cost*.json` — the
  evidence to link from the PR body
- `RUNBOOK.md` — where the pre-filled PR form goes while the ticket waits for a
  slot, following PR B's precedent

## Actions

1. Add `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_DTYPE` to the config
   schema, optional, a plain pass-through string. Do NOT enumerate the valid
   values in a local allowlist that will rot against the runtime's enum.
2. Pass `device: 'auto'` unconditionally. It is NOT configurable, and there is
   no `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_DEVICE` (author's ruling, 2026-08-29). See "Why device is
   not a knob" above for what `auto` resolves to and why it needs no escape
   hatch.
3. Thread dtype into `pipeline('feature-extraction', model, opts)`, building the
   options object so that an unset variable produces an ABSENT `dtype` key
   rather than `undefined`.
4. Make dtype part of the vector-space identity; do NOT make device part of it.
   Two dtypes of one model do not produce interchangeable vectors, so the
   embedder key must distinguish them or a dtype switch silently mixes spaces in
   one index. Device is different in kind: the same graph at the same precision
   on a different execution provider differs only in the last bits, far inside
   `cboulanger/zotero-rag`'s 0,999 compatibility gate, so putting device in the
   key would invalidate every vector whenever a GPU appears or goes away.
   Confirm that with the 0,999 probe before relying on it.
5. Degrade, do not abort, when a DTYPE will not load. `fp16` and `q4f16` throw at
   session init on the CPU provider; catch it, name the offending value and the
   model, and fall back to the runtime default with a warning rather than taking
   the server down. This does not apply to device, which self-heals (below).
6. Document the one variable where `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_MODEL` is documented, with
   the measured ladder linked and NO recommended value. Say that the device is
   chosen automatically and is not configurable.
7. Do not open the PR yet. Build it on a fork branch, validate, and add the
   pre-filled form to RUNBOOK.md. At filing there was no free slot: the in-flight
   count stood above the volume bound on a granted exception, and the
   contained-PR budget had a live remainder. Do not re-read those numbers from
   this ticket — the bounds are `GOVERNANCE.md`'s and what is live against them
   is `SYNC.md`'s, and both move. Check them before opening. This queues behind a
   free slot exactly as 0016's PR-3 does.

## Test

Red first, and it is the test that would have caught the defect this ticket is
about: with no environment set, the options object handed to `pipeline()` must
carry `device: 'auto'` and NO `dtype` key at all. It fails against today's
no-options call (no device), and it fails against the naive implementation that
passes `{dtype: undefined}`. Those are two different wrong answers and the one
assertion rejects both.

Then, using the existing injectable `loadExtractor` seam so no model is
downloaded in the unit tier:

- `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_DTYPE=q8` reaches `pipeline()` as `{dtype: 'q8', device:
  'auto'}`
- no environment variable, and no combination of them, can produce a `device`
  other than `'auto'` — assert on the absence of the knob, since a knob nobody
  added is exactly the thing a later well-meaning patch adds back
- an extractor factory that throws an init-time error yields a warning and a
  retry with default options, not a propagated throw (action 5)
- two dtypes of the same model produce different embedder keys, and the key is
  unchanged across devices (action 4)

Integration tier, marked accordingly: one real load at `q8` asserting the
returned dimension, so the pass-through is proven against the actual runtime and
not only against a mock that cannot disagree.

## Verification

- [x] Unset-environment test fails BOTH ways it can be wrong. All three run:
      against today's no-options call it fails ("expected undefined to be
      defined", in a worktree at b132f2d); against a `{dtype: this.activeDtype}`
      implementation it fails ("expected true to be false", the `'dtype' in
      options` assertion); it passes against the shipped one
- [x] `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_DTYPE=q8` measurably changes resident memory on a real
      load. Measured on the DEFAULT model, which the ladder above does not
      cover: all-MiniLM-L6-v2 goes 143,7 MB → 69,1 MB of resident added by the
      load, 230,6 → 165,6 MB resident after it. Direction matches; magnitude is
      2,1x where nomic's was 3,8x, which is what a smaller model should do.
      Warm runs — the cold pair measured the download and is discarded
- [x] An invalid dtype degrades rather than aborting — but neither via the value
      nor via the mechanism this ticket named. `q7` does not abort at all: it
      loads bit-identical to the default, so the runtime ignores it silently and
      the example was wrong. And the mechanism had to change: serving at the
      default precision is unsafe here, because the index stamps the embedder
      identity before it embeds (see the 2026-08-29 review log). An unloadable
      value now throws into `noteEmbedFailure`, so the index degrades to
      keyword-only with the value, the model and the remedy named — the server
      stays up, which was action 5's actual intent
- [x] `device: 'auto'` on a CPU-only machine behaves exactly as today's path —
      FALSIFIED, and this is the ticket's main finding. It does not behave as
      today's path; it fails to load at all. `device: 'cpu'` and today's
      no-options call are the two that agree, bit for bit
- [ ] `device: 'auto'` on a CUDA machine actually selects CUDA, and its vectors
      score >= 0,999 cosine against the CPU vectors for the same model and
      dtype. NOTE: UNVERIFIED and unverifiable here — this repo's machine has no
      GPU (`nvidia-smi` absent), so no device value has ever been executed. Every
      GPU claim in this ticket is read from `src/backends/onnx.js`, not observed.
      Run it on the A4000 before the PR asserts anything about GPU behaviour
- [x] Upstream's own test suite still passes unchanged: 856 passed / 7 skipped
      against a 847 / 7 baseline on b132f2d, so the 9 added are the whole
      difference; typecheck, lint and build clean

## Invariants

- Behaviour with `ZOTEUS_EMBEDDING_DTYPE` unset is identical to today ON A
  CPU-ONLY MACHINE. It is deliberately NOT identical on a machine with a GPU,
  which is the point of the ruling; that is the single intended behaviour change
  and it is stated in the PR body rather than left for a user to discover
- Device is never configurable and never enters the embedder key
- R10 holds: the local path still makes exactly one external call, the one-time
  model-weight download, and a dtype failure degrades toward keyword-only, never
  toward an API embedder
- No recommended dtype default ships in this PR, in code or in prose
- The contained-PR budget and the in-flight cap are respected: built and queued,
  not opened

## Exit criteria

> **THE REMEDY IS WITHDRAWN, 2026-08-29 (author).** No precision knob is offered
> upstream. The criteria below are kept as the record of what was built and
> measured, not as work outstanding — every box that names the upstream filing is
> void, and `tickets/0440` carries the ask that replaces it. This ticket now
> closes on its evidence being recorded, which it is.

